                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[b]My score: 5.5/10\n⭐ TL;DR:[/b] A fun and ridiculous roguelite with plenty of characters, weapons and modifiers, but repetitive content and game-breaking bugs make it hard to recommend.\n\n🟥 [b]NOTE:[/b] I can't recommend this game in its current state. Several known bugs significantly interfere with the intended gameplay, including one that can make a character virtually invincible. Some of these issues have been known for quite some time, and I couldn't find any recent indication that the game is still being actively maintained.\n\n[h2]Overview[/h2]\nA fairly standard roguelite with a fun and completely bonkers premise: an asteroid has fallen to Earth, turning fruit sentient and causing it to rebel against humanity. Your job is to go around slaughtering pears, bananas, peaches and whatever else nature has decided to weaponize.\n\nThe game clearly descends from [i]The Binding of Isaac[/i]'s branch of the roguelite family, minus the darker tone and imagery. The roster of characters and weapons is varied enough to keep things interesting for quite a few hours. Unfortunately, the same can't be said for the maps and enemies, which become repetitive and occasionally rely on some genuinely annoying mechanics.\n\nThere's also a rather unfortunate elephant in the room: I quickly discovered that one of the characters is effectively invincible due to a bug. This completely removes any semblance of challenge, allowing me to breeze through the highest difficulty and experiment with pretty much every build I wanted.\n\n\"But if you know about the bug, why not just use another character?\" Fair point. But once you know the game offers you an opportunity to play it in this flawed state, especially when you're hunting achievements or trying to unlock things, it becomes hard not to take advantage of it. I'd feel silly deliberately handicapping myself when I know there's a way around the grind. The downside is that the game consequently loses much of its appeal as a challenge.\n\nIt's still fun, especially in couch co-op, but even while playing with another person and taking advantage of the invincibility bug, I found the overall experience surprisingly grindy.\n\nhttps://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3833839299\n[h2]One Thing I Liked / One Thing I Didn’t[/h2]\n✔️ [b]Liked:[/b] The completely unhinged sense of humor behind some of the weapons.\n✖️ [b]Didn't:[/b] Some of the enemy mechanics. I have developed a genuine fear of pomegranates.\n\n[h2]Key Points[/h2]\n[*][h3]DIFFICULTY:[/h3] You can unlock difficulty modifiers fairly early, allowing you to make the game easier or harder by adding or removing handicaps: zigzagging enemy projectiles, restricted field of view, limited health, and so on. Achievement hunters beware: you'll eventually have to play with all the difficulty modifiers active at the same time.\n[*][h3]PRESENTATION:[/h3] The graphical style is pleasant, if a little sparse and derivative of more famous works... The actual character and enemy design quality is all over the place: some designs are genuinely good, while others look suspiciously close to placeholders. The final boss, in particular, looks almost unfinished. Sound effects are cute, but they can get tiring after a while.\n[*][h3]STORY:[/h3] There is a story, but it's deliberately silly and never gets in the way.\n[*][h3]GAMEPLAY/GAMEPLAY LOOP:[/h3] A fairly standard 2D roguelite loop that becomes considerably more enjoyable in multiplayer.\n[*][h3]TECHNICAL POLISH:[/h3] Unfortunately, the game is affected by several visual glitches and some significant bugs, including the game-breaking invincibility bug mentioned above. These issues have a real impact on the intended experience.\n[*][h3]MONEYxCONTENT VALUE:[/h3] I played for 18 hours at a €10 price point, so by my usual \"more content for the money is better\" rule, the game passes with flying colors. I don't mean €1 = exactly 1 hour—it's simply my shorthand for judging how much value a game offers relative to its price. If this game were regularly updated and its bugs fixed, I'd consider it worth every cent. In its current state, however, the lack of maintenance makes it difficult to recommend.\n[/list]",
